\details{
\strong{Long format} has one row per respondent per wave, with a \code{"Wave"}
column (the LASA wave code, e.g. \code{"B"}, \code{"2B"}, \code{"MB"}) and a \code{"Time"}
column right after \code{respnr}. Each wave-specific variable becomes one
column named after its wave-stripped (canonical) name, so \code{boak}, \code{coak},
... become \code{oak}. A respondent gets a row for each wave at which at least
one of the wave-specific columns has a value for them -- anything but
\code{NA} or blank text (which is how SPSS stores missing text), so a
missing-value code kept as a value (read with
\code{read_lasa_sav(keep_user_na = TRUE)}) counts too. Respondents of a
later cohort therefore only get rows for the waves before they joined
LASA where the data hold such a code for them, as LASA's Z files do
("no observation birth cohorts 1938-1947"). A respondent without any
value at any wave keeps a row for every wave, so nobody disappears.
Rows are sorted by respondent (in order of first appearance) and wave,
except in long data without wide columns, which keep their order.

\strong{Time} counts the waves since the respondent's baseline, the wave at
which their cohort joined LASA, which is Time 0. A cohort takes part in
every later wave except the other cohorts' baselines:
\itemize{
\item first cohort: B = 0, C = 1, D = 2, E = 3, F = 4, G = 5, H = 6, I = 7,
J = 8, K = 9, L = 10;
\item second cohort: 2B = 0, F = 1, G = 2, H = 3, I = 4, J = 5, K = 6, L = 7;
\item third cohort and migrant cohort: 3B or MB = 0, I = 1, J = 2, K = 3,
L = 4;
\item fourth cohort: 4B = 0, L = 1.
}

Wave A, the LSN pilot study a few months before wave B, isn't part of any
cohort's waves: its rows keep \code{"Wave"} \code{"A"} and have no Time (\code{NA}).
Time counts waves, not years, and it isn't LASA's own wave numbering,
which \code{LASAZ008.SAV} uses (\code{t2_dat} is wave B there); for time itself,
use the interview dates or ages in \code{LASAZ008.SAV}.

A row's Time depends on the respondent's cohort, which the data show by:
\itemize{
\item a value (other than \code{NA}, blank text, or a missing-value code) at the
respondent's baseline or at a wave only their cohort takes part in (B
to E for the first cohort);
\item a single LASA file of such a wave, whose respondents all belong to its
cohort, whatever their values (the data's \code{"LASA_file_code"} and
\code{"LASA_wave"} attributes say which file; not a Z file, whose rows may
hold codes for waves before a cohort joined): in long data all of whose
rows are of that wave, any row, unless it holds nothing but values a
guess put at its wave (see \strong{Names of several waves}); in wide data
(the file read with LASA's own names, \code{standardize = FALSE} in
\code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}}, or transformed to wide format), every respondent,
as long as all columns are the file's (those its label report lists,
and the names wide format gives columns of its wave) -- once other
data are joined on, every respondent with a value in one of the file's
columns (one the join added has none), also when the file in wide data
was joined onto long data, keeping its attributes (as \code{dplyr}'s joins
do with it first);
\item LASA's \code{cohort} column, as \code{LASAZ002.SAV} and Time-prefixed wide data
(see \strong{Wide format}) have it: \code{"cohort 1 (1992)"} -- also written
\code{"Cohort 1"} or \code{"cohort 1 (1992/1993)"} -- or its code 1 is the first
cohort, whose baseline is B; cohort 2's is 2B, cohort 3's 3B, cohort
4's 4B, and the \code{"migrant cohort"}'s MB;
\item a \code{"Time"} column already in long data;
\item the respondent number: LASA numbers its respondents with five digits,
the first of which is 1, 2, or 3 in the first cohort, 4, 5, or 6 in
the second cohort, and 7, 8, or 9 in the third. A 1 also numbers the
LSN pilot study's respondents, so it only says the first cohort of a
respondent the data show at a wave other than A, by a value there
(rows that hold nothing but missing-value codes don't count). The
fourth and the migrant cohort's numbers aren't known yet: if they have
five digits too, they say one of the first three cohorts until the
rest of the data show their own (their baseline file, say, or a
\code{cohort} column). A number of any other form (the longer numbers the
fourth cohort may have, or the small numbers of the examples below)
says nothing. Where the rest of the data show another cohort than a
respondent's number, they decide, with a warning in long and
Time-prefixed wide format, which show the cohort -- also over a
\code{"Time"} column that follows the number (as \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} gives
it at waves several cohorts share).
}

A missing-value code is what \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} turns into \code{NA} unless
\code{keep_user_na = TRUE}: an SPSS user-missing value; in a variable the
label database documents, any negative code, documented or not, except
in a variable whose values can be negative at that wave (such as the
speech-reception thresholds \code{msrttn}), where only its documented codes
are; and in any column, a negative code with a value label -- also as
the text or factor level read_lasa_sav() gives it (its label, or its own
number). Values show the cohort in the wave-specific variables the label
database documents (and long columns \code{transform_lasa_data()} made from
wide columns); in long data without any, in the columns whose values
differ between a respondent's rows (a variable computed per wave, say;
an \code{NA} counts as a value of its own), or else in all columns but the
documented stable variables; and in long data without such columns,
every row shows its wave. The waves from F on, which cohorts share,
don't show the cohort: the rows of a respondent seen only at those waves
get no Time, with a message, unless their respondent number or another
of the sources above shows it (or every cohort the data leave open gives
their wave the same Time). Data showing waves of several cohorts for one
respondent give that respondent no Time, with a warning.

A \code{"Time"} column already in long data -- also the \code{Time.x}/\code{Time.y}
columns \code{\link[=merge]{merge()}} makes of two, and the further copies merging more data
sets gives (\code{Time.x.x}, or several of the same name) -- must give each
row a Time that the respondent's cohort, as far as the rest of the data
show it, gives its wave, and copies must agree with each other, except
that a copy following the respondent number gives way to one that
doesn't (that of a file of a wave several cohorts share read on its
own, next to that of data that showed another cohort, say); it is
replaced by the recomputed one.

A \code{cohort} column (in any capitalization) is LASA's when its values,
factor levels, or value labels name cohorts the way above, or when it
holds nothing but their codes (and missing values): LASA's codes 1 to 4,
and 5 for the migrant cohort, as Time-prefixed wide data code it, as
numbers, or as text or factor levels (\code{"4"}, the way \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}}
reads a code the label database doesn't document) -- unless value labels
say they are something else (birth cohorts, say; labels of negative
missing-value codes aside). In a column whose value labels name cohorts,
a code without a value label names its cohort too, as does one labelled
with its own number (the level \code{"4"} in a \code{.sav} file). Any other
\code{cohort} column is an ordinary column. LASA's cohort column must give each
respondent one cohort, which the waves the data show must fit; values
that name no cohort (a missing-value code) say nothing. In long format,
it stays as it is; coded as Time-prefixed wide data have it (as
\code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} reads \code{LASAZ002.SAV}'s), it gets back the labels
\code{\link[=merge]{merge()}} and row subsetting drop. Several columns named \code{cohort} -- the
\code{cohort.x}/\code{cohort.y} columns of data sets with a \code{cohort} column merged
by \code{respnr} (\code{LASAZ002.SAV}, Time-prefixed wide data), the further
copies merging more of them gives (\code{cohort.x.x}, or several of the same
name), and columns whose names differ in capitalization only
(\code{LASAZ002.SAV}'s \code{COHORT} as \code{\link[haven:read_spss]{haven::read_sav()}} reads it, next to
Time-prefixed wide data's \code{cohort}) -- are combined into one \code{cohort},
coded as Time-prefixed wide data have it, and must agree. A copy that
names no cohort is another data set's own column, and stays as it is; a
column named \code{cohort} that names none, next to one that does, is an
error.

\strong{Stable variables} -- columns that hold for every wave, such as sex or
year of birth (\code{byear}) in \code{LASAZ004.SAV} -- keep one column and are
repeated on each of the respondent's rows: in long data, a stable
variable the label database documents is filled in on the respondent's
rows where it is missing (rows other files' columns added, or rows of
other waves merged in by \code{respnr} and \code{"Wave"}) from their other rows,
as long as those agree. A file without wave-specific columns, such as
\code{LASAZ004.SAV} or the migrant cohort's \code{LASMB004.SAV}, has no wave
dimension: it stays one row per respondent, without \code{"Wave"} and
\code{"Time"} columns, with a message.

\strong{Which columns are wave-specific} is decided per column: a column made
wide by \code{transform_lasa_data()} itself is recognized by its name, or by
attributes it carries where its name doesn't say enough (see \strong{Wide
format}); any other column is wave-specific when \code{\link[=lasa_label_db]{lasa_label_db()}}
documents its name as a wave-specific variable -- which covers LASA's
irregular names too, such as \code{t2_dat} (\code{t_dat}, wave B), \code{b_dm} (\code{dm}),
\code{b2oak} (\code{oak}, wave 2B), or \code{totalalpha}, which LASA's wave H file uses
without a wave prefix, and a column \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} matched to such a
name by fuzzy matching or \code{name_corrections} -- or, for data that aren't
long yet (no \code{"Wave"} column, or one holding nothing but the \code{"Z"}
placeholder \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} gives a Z file and the missing values
merging with that file gives -- only missing values, too, in data with
one row per respondent), when its name is a wave prefix followed by a
variable the label database documents for that wave (the names wide
format uses, see below), by any wave-specific variable it documents if
the wave is 4B or L, which it doesn't document yet (\code{lhhnch}), or by a
variable LASA's wave files name without a wave prefix if the wave isn't
A (\code{kseh_reden}). All other columns are
treated as stable, including columns the label database doesn't know. A
warning names columns kept stable that look like wave-specific
variables: names a merge suffixed with \code{.x}/\code{.y}, and, in wide data,
variables read with their wave stripped from their name.

In long data, a column named after a variable (its long-format name) is
that variable's long column, whose values stay at their rows' waves.
So is a column of a wave-specific name whose values are all on rows of
waves that name stands for: the waves the label database documents it
at, and the wave of the LASA file the data are (as far as the data's
label report lists the column) -- also where the label database doesn't
document the name at that wave, as for the files of waves 4B and L,
which it doesn't document yet -- and, for a name LASA's wave files use
without a wave prefix (\code{drinking}), those undocumented waves, any wave
once its values differ between a respondent's rows, and, in the data of
that name's LASA file (as far as the data's label report lists the
column; stacked files keep the first one's), the waves \code{\link[=lasa_topics]{lasa_topics()}}
lists the file at that the label database doesn't document of it yet
(wave K for file 045's \code{seh_reden}), unless the file's wave-prefixed
columns are wide there (a wide file joined onto other long data). In the
data of a LASA file of wave L, a column the file's label report lists
named \code{l} followed by a wave-specific variable (\code{lhhnch}) is that variable's
long column too, so wave L's files become long as other waves' do. Wave
I's \code{immse01} to \code{immse23} are also the long names of the migrant
cohort's \code{bimmse01} to \code{bimmse23}: such a column is the migrant cohort's
long column when its values are on rows of wave MB (or its
\code{canonical_name} attribute says so), and wave I's column otherwise.

\strong{Names of several waves.} LASA's wave files name the baseline of every
cohort (B, 2B, 3B, MB, and 4B) with the same \code{b} prefix, so a name such
as \code{blphya01} may belong to any of them, and a few other names stand for
several waves too (\code{bqvalu16} for waves 3B and J, \code{xcancer1} for waves B
and C). Each respondent's value in such a column goes to the wave the
data show for that respondent: in long data, a value already on a row of
one of those waves stays there; otherwise, the respondent's cohort, as
far as the data (or else the respondent number) show it (the first
cohort has no wave 3B, so its respondents' \code{bqvalu16} is wave J's), and
then the file the column came from (the data's \code{"LASA_file_code"} and
\code{"LASA_wave"} attributes, as far as the data's label report lists the
column, since a merge keeps at most the first file's attributes). If
nothing says, the value goes, with a message naming the column, to the
first of these waves the respondent's cohort may take part in: the wave
the column's other respondents were placed at by their cohort, the wave
of the data's \code{"LASA_wave"} attribute, and the column's waves in
calendar order (and
for a name LASA's wave files use without a wave prefix, \code{drinking}, if
the respondent's cohort takes part in none of those: waves 4B and L,
which the label database doesn't document yet). Such a guess doesn't
count as showing the respondent's cohort, so it gives none of their
rows a Time unless something else, such as their respondent number,
shows the cohort (which may leave several of the column's waves open:
the third cohort takes part in both of \code{bqvalu16}'s) -- also when the
result is transformed again: the long
column remembers which of its values were placed by a guess, and wide
format gives those values one column, also when the guesses put them at
several waves, under a name that doesn't say the wave (its original
name, or else LASA's own, such as \code{blphya01}), so transforming that back
guesses again, with the message. The long column keeps that memory in
its attributes, which \code{\link[=rbind]{rbind()}}, \code{\link[=merge]{merge()}}, \code{dplyr::bind_rows()},
subsetting or sorting the rows of a data frame (rather than a tibble),
and writing the data to a \code{.sav} file can drop: after that, those values
count as showing the cohort of the wave they were put at. To avoid
guesses, transform each LASA file to long format before combining files
(with \code{format_as_long = TRUE} in \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}}).

One name stands for two variables at one wave: \code{bmmsesc1} is file 221's
\code{mmsesc} and file 421's \code{mmsesc1} at wave MB. The data's file (or the
column's \code{canonical_name} attribute) says which; otherwise it is taken
as the variable the name stands for at its other waves (\code{mmsesc1}), with
a message.

\strong{Merged files.} \code{data} may be several LASA files merged together, in
any of three shapes:
\itemize{
\item long (the most reliable): files read with \code{format_as_long = TRUE},
which gives each file's rows their Time while the file still shows
their cohort (in a baseline file, also rows holding nothing but
missing-value codes), stacked per file code with \code{dplyr::bind_rows()}
(or \code{\link[=rbind]{rbind()}}, if the files have the same columns) and merged by
\code{respnr} and \code{"Wave"} (with \code{all = TRUE}, to keep the rows of
respondents missing from some files); the \code{Time.x} and \code{Time.y}
columns that merge makes become one \code{"Time"} again, now also for the
rows of files that didn't show the respondent's cohort on their own.
Stacking and merging drop the columns' labels (or keep one file's for
all rows), which \code{transform_lasa_data()} gives back (see \strong{Labels}).
A file without wave-specific columns (\code{LASAZ004.SAV}, \code{LASMB004.SAV})
read with \code{format_as_long = TRUE} has no \code{"Wave"} column: merge it by
\code{respnr} alone;
\item wide, e.g. \code{LASAB046.SAV}, \code{LASAC046.SAV}, \code{LASAB030.SAV}, ... read
with \code{standardize = FALSE} and merged by \code{respnr} (with \code{all = TRUE}),
or a Z file merged with \code{LASAZ004.SAV}. \code{\link[=merge]{merge()}} drops the files'
attributes (\code{dplyr}'s joins keep the first data set's), so the values
in \code{b} columns of a respondent the data show nowhere else (one who
dropped out after wave B, say) go to the baseline their respondent
number says, or else are placed by a guess (see \strong{Names of several
waves}), which gives them no Time. So merge the baseline files of the
migrant and the fourth cohort (\code{LASMB046.SAV}, \code{LAS4B046.SAV}), whose
respondent numbers aren't known yet, in long format. Don't stack
wide files of different cohorts that share column names (e.g.
\code{LAS2B046.SAV} below \code{LASAB046.SAV}, or \code{LASAZDC2.SAV} below
\code{LASAZDC1.SAV}): stack them in long format;
\item a mix: long data that wide columns were merged into by \code{respnr}, for
example a Z file's -- without the placeholder \code{"Wave"} column
\code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} gives a Z file, or the two \code{"Wave"} columns end up as
\code{Wave.x} and \code{Wave.y}, which is an error. \code{format = "long"} moves each
wide column's values to its wave's row, adding a row where a
respondent has a value at a wave the long data don't have a row for
yet. On those added rows, stable variables the label database
documents (such as \code{sex}) are filled in from the respondent's other
rows, and other columns are left missing, since they weren't measured
at that wave.
}

\strong{Wide format} has one row per respondent and drops the \code{"Wave"} and
\code{"Time"} columns. Each wave-specific variable gets one column per wave
at which it has any value, and a long column made from wide columns gets
all of those columns back, also ones without any value. With
\code{prefix = "Wave"}, a column's name is its wave's lowercase LASA prefix
followed by the variable name: \code{boak}, \code{coak}, \code{doak}, ... Waves 2B, 3B,
4B, and MB get the prefixes \code{b2}, \code{b3}, \code{b4}, and \code{mb} (as LASA's own
\code{zoa2}/\code{zoa3} files do for 2B and 3B), so they don't collide with wave
B's columns. Where LASA's own name for the variable at that wave stands
for nothing else, the column gets that name instead (\code{m15wrth} is
\code{bm15twtrh} at wave B and \code{c15wtrth} at wave C; \code{totalalpha}, wave H's
name without a wave prefix, stays \code{totalalpha}). A column that came from
a wide LASA file gets its original name back (e.g. \code{t2_dat} rather than
\code{bt_dat}), unless its values went to several waves or the name doesn't
say its wave by itself: the \code{b_dm} column of \code{LASAZDC2.SAV}, which holds
the second cohort's baseline, becomes \code{b2dm}.

With \code{prefix = "Time"}, names are \code{t}, the \code{"Time"}, and an underscore
followed by the variable name: \code{t0_oak} holds each respondent's value at
their own baseline, \code{t1_oak} at their cohort's next wave (wave C for the
first cohort, F for the second), and so on, so the cohorts share
columns. A \code{cohort} column after \code{respnr} gives each respondent's
cohort, which transforming the data back to long format uses. It is
LASA's cohort variable the way \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} reads \code{LASAZ002.SAV}'s
-- a factor of its value labels (\code{"cohort 1 (1992)"}, \code{"cohort 2 (2002)"}, \code{"cohort 3 (2012)"}), with its labels -- so the result merges
with that file by \code{respnr} and \code{cohort}, read with \code{to_factor = TRUE}
(the default); read with \code{to_factor = FALSE}, merge it by \code{respnr}
alone (the \code{cohort.x} and \code{cohort.y} columns that gives are combined,
see above). The fourth cohort, whose code 4 the label database doesn't
document yet, gets the level \code{"4"}, as \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} reads that
code, and the migrant cohort, which \code{LASAZ002.SAV} doesn't have, the
level \code{"migrant cohort"} after it. These levels come where the data have
respondents of these cohorts -- \code{"4"} also with only the migrant
cohort's -- so that each cohort keeps its code (1 to 5) when the factor
becomes numbers (in a \code{.sav} file, say). The column replaces a \code{cohort}
column \code{data} have, which must then be LASA's: any other column named
\code{cohort} (in any capitalization) is an error, so rename it first. Wave
A, which has no Time, keeps its own column (\code{aoak}). Every other value
needs a Time: values at a wave without one for their respondent are an
error, except missing-value codes, which are left out with a message. A
respondent whose rows have a Time while the data leave their cohort
open (rows of waves I to L only, which the third and the migrant cohort
give the same Time, of a respondent whose number doesn't say the
cohort, or says one the data rule out) gets a missing \code{cohort}, with a
warning: their columns can't be transformed back, also not by their
respondent number. A column holding several cohorts' waves (\code{t0_oak}: B,
2B, ...) gets those waves' types, value labels and user-missing codes,
combined the way long format combines waves, with a warning where their
user-missing codes don't combine, and their \code{"wave_label"}/\code{"labels_wave"}
as one entry per wave.
Original names aren't kept with \code{prefix = "Time"}, also not when such
data are made wide again with \code{prefix = "Wave"}.

A column stays stable in wide format when it has a single value per
respondent and isn't a documented wave-specific variable; a documented
stable variable (such as \code{sex}) only needs to agree across the rows
where it isn't missing. A wide column whose name still doesn't say which
wave and variable it holds (a name the label database doesn't document,
or a wave B name the later cohorts' baselines share, such as
\code{blphya01}, and, in a result with wave 4B, whose files use the
baselines' \code{b} names, any baseline's \code{b} name) carries the attributes
\code{"LASA_wave"} and \code{"LASA_long_name"}, which transforming it back uses,
also without the result's other columns. Base R's \code{\link[=merge]{merge()}} and row
subsetting drop those attributes from factor and numeric columns
(haven's labelled columns keep them); the column is then placed like a
column of LASA's own files, see \strong{Names of several waves}.

\strong{Labels} travel with the data. A long column keeps each wave's own
\code{"wave_label"}/\code{"labels_wave"} as one entry per wave. Its variable label
is the one its waves share, or else their harmonized (cross-wave)
label, or else the first wave's. Its value labels are those of all
waves combined; if a code is labelled differently at different waves,
it gets the harmonized value labels, or none. SPSS user-missing codes
(\code{"na_values"}, \code{"na_range"}) are combined as long as that marks exactly
the same values missing on every wave's rows; otherwise the long column
gets none, with a warning. Factor levels are combined across waves;
wave-specific columns whose types don't combine (e.g. a factor at one
wave and numbers at another) become text, without value labels or
user-missing codes, with a warning. Time spans (\code{difftime}) get a unit
every wave's values convert to exactly, the first wave's if possible;
date-times that become text are written in UTC
(\code{"2021-10-31 00:30:00+0000"}), so they read back as the same moment.
Converting back to wide restores each wide column's original type (also
from text), attributes, and user-missing codes, as long as its values
still fit them.

In long data, the column of a wave-specific variable the label database
documents that has lost its labels -- \code{\link[=rbind]{rbind()}}, \code{dplyr::bind_rows()}
and \code{\link[=merge]{merge()}} drop them from factor and numeric columns, and \code{\link[=rbind]{rbind()}}
keeps one file's for all rows of labelled columns -- gets back the
labels \code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} gives the variable at each wave it has values
at, from the label database: \code{"wave_label"}/\code{"labels_wave"} with an
entry per wave, \code{"canonical_name"}, the harmonized label and value
labels where the database has them, and as \code{"label"} and \code{"labels"}
the harmonized ones, or else the waves' shared (or first) wording and
all their value labels. The data then get a \code{"variable.labels"}
attribute too, if they had none. So does a long column made of wide
columns that have all lost their labels (wide files merged with
\code{\link[=merge]{merge()}}); wide format gives those columns back as they were. Labels
of a column's own (not ones the database gives the variable, such as a
user's own wording) are kept as they are, and a column with
\code{"wave_label"} entries for some of its waves only gets the other
waves', as long as those it has are the database's. A wave at which the
database documents the variable with different labels in several files
(\code{partner} at wave MB) gets none.
}
\examples{
# A Z-file-shaped data frame: knee osteoarthritis (oak) of two
# respondents of the first cohort (waves B, C, and F) and one of the
# second (waves 2B and F), plus a stable variable (sex).
wide <- data.frame(
  respnr = c(101, 102, 201),
  sex = c(1, 2, 2),
  boak = c(0, 2, NA),
  coak = c(1, 8, NA),
  b2oak = c(NA, NA, 1),
  foak = c(2, NA, 0)
)

# format = "long" is the default. Time counts the waves since each
# respondent's own baseline: wave F is Time 4 for the first cohort, but
# Time 1 for the second.
long <- transform_lasa_data(wide)
long

# And back again, with wave letters or Time numbers as prefixes:
transform_lasa_data(long, format = "wide")
transform_lasa_data(long, format = "wide", prefix = "Time")

\dontrun{
# Read a Z file straight into long format:
oa <- read_lasa_sav("LASAZOA1.SAV", format_as_long = TRUE)
}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=read_lasa_sav]{read_lasa_sav()}} (\code{format_as_long}), \code{\link[=apply_lasa_labels]{apply_lasa_labels()}},
\code{\link[=lasa_label_db]{lasa_label_db()}}
}
